    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d647">

                <pb facs="0634" n="630" sameAs="frus:frus1938v03/pg630"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">693.002/450: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Consul General at Shanghai</hi> (<hi
                        rendition="#italic">Gauss</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of
                        State</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Shanghai</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1938-01-14T19:00:00+08:00">January 14, 1938—7
                        p.m.</date><lb/>[Received January 14—4:45 p.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>78. Minister of Finance<anchor n="37" xml:id="d647-f1"/> has telegraphed
                    Inspector General of Customs expressing appreciation of efforts of the
                    commissioners concerned in maintaining the <hi rendition="#italic">status
                        quo</hi> of the Tientsin and Chinwangtao customs but stating that, (1), no
                    orders should be accepted from and no agreement of any kind should be negotiated
                    and concluded with <pb facs="0635" n="631" xml:id="pg631"/> the bogus
                    government; (2), no changes should be made in the present customs system rules
                    and regulations; (3), present tariffs should not be revised without authority,
                    these three conditions being the minimum that should be held in maintaining the
                    Customs Administration and under no circumstances be compromised. Commissioners
                    of Customs to be instructed accordingly.</p>
                <p>2. Inspector General has replied that the commissioners are being instructed,
                    that no written agreement whatever has been made with the bogus government, that
                    in order to maintain sovereign rights and credit at home and abroad by
                    preserving the integrity of the service as the Government’s agents throughout
                    China, et cetera, it may become advisable at some ports to yield to <hi
                        rendition="#italic">force majeure</hi> in nonessentials, and that to
                    stimulate trade and help revive economic conditions seriously upset by
                    hostilities, the Inspector General may later submit some tariff proposals for
                    approval of the Government.</p>
                <p>Repeated to Hankow and Peiping.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Gauss</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
